<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Language Model Architecture</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employed Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, a 7.24 billion parameter model organized across 32 computational layers with advanced features including Grouped-Query Attention for focused information processing and Rotary Position Embedding enabling analysis of up to 25,000 words of clinical text. While larger models like GPT-4 demonstrate superior benchmarks, we prioritized practical ICU deployment requirements. Mistral offers critical advantages: local deployment within hospital IT infrastructure ensuring patient data privacy, computational efficiency enabling real-time processing on standard servers, and operational feasibility without external API dependencies. Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Language Model Architecture ‣ 2 Materials and Methods ‣ Aligning Language Models with Clinical Expertise: DPO for Heart Failure Nursing Documentation in Critical Care" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates how Mistral’s characteristics align with ICU requirements where data confidentiality, low latency, and cost-effectiveness are paramount.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of language models for ICU clinical deployment scenarios.</figcaption>
